answer:
SYTRD sets pointer mode to host because it uses scalar constants (minone = -1, one = 1) that are stored on the host stack. The code:

```cpp
rocblas_set_pointer_mode(handle, rocblas_pointer_mode_host);
const T minone = T(-1);
const T one = T(1);
```

These scalars are passed to rocBLAS GEMM calls. With host pointer mode, rocBLAS reads the scalar values from host memory (the stack) rather than expecting device pointers.

This is more convenient than allocating device memory for two constants. The mode is restored at the end: `rocblas_set_pointer_mode(handle, old_mode)`.

Note: SYTD2/HETD2 uses device pointer mode because it has scalars pre-allocated in device memory (the 'scalars' array).

